 The board's PCH and PHY state, read and painted, for the I219 campaign
 (`docs/Designs/Active/OS/I219IsNotAnE1000.md`). It is a MEASUREMENT stage and
 it is the whole payload of sitting 7 (red, 2026-08-21): no driver change flies
 with it, and it asserts nothing. Eight hypotheses cost a few registers each and
 the campaign's first rule is that one boot answers all of them rather than one
 per trip, because the variables were made serial and Damian has been to the
 machine about 150 times.

 IT WRITES ONE REGISTER AND THAT IS NOT A DRIVER CHANGE. PHY registers above 15
 are paged and register 31 is the page selector (I219 rev 2.02 section 9.3), so
 a paged register cannot be read without selecting its page. The page is
 restored to 0 afterwards. Nothing else here writes anything.

 WHAT IS CITED AND WHAT IS NOT, per line, because on this campaign that
 distinction is the point and a number without its status is worse than no
 number. reek holds the datasheets and gave these with their provenance
 (`I219IsNotAnE1000.md`, main 18349).

Section: What we can read and what we cannot

 FWSM IS NOT PAINTED AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. reek: "I cannot give a cited
 offset and will not give an uncited one." The string appears in neither
 datasheet in this tree, I219 or 82583V, zero matches; every offset quotable
 for it comes from the vendor driver, which is the one source this campaign
 forbids. So the row says the gap out loud. A measurement flight that paints a
 number read from a guessed offset is worse than one that paints nothing there:
 it returns a confident wrong reading that nobody can tell from a right one.

  dpch-states : List Text = ["ok", "no-mdio", "no-part", "rejected", "bar-bad"]

 EVERY READING HAS BEEN SHOWN TO MOVE, and that is the gate this stage had to
 pass. red, 2026-08-21: a measurement payload asserts nothing the bed could
 falsify, so it is exempt from the campaign's fly-gate pair, but it is NOT
 exempt from proving its own instrument. Every register painted here must read
 TWO DIFFERENT values in the bed, because one that always paints 0000 looks
 exactly like a board with K1 off.

 ONE ROW FAILS THAT GATE AND SHIPS ANYWAY, DECLARED. The kmrn row below cannot
 move in this bed for a structural reason rather than a missing arm, and the
 table says so on its own line instead of omitting it. A gate with a silent
 exception is worse than one with a stated exception: the silent version leaves
 the table asserting something false about a row a reader is trusting.

 WHAT WAS PROVEN INSTEAD, because "cannot move" must not become "never checked".
 The bed shows the row DECLINES rather than guesses: with the bracket silent it
 answers SAYS NOTHING, so a bed reading can never be mistaken for the board's.
 The decision itself was then ablated on fixtures, 2026-08-21, and all four
 branches are reachable and distinct: a value at 0x00034 gives DECODES,
 all-ones gives UNCLAIMED, everything silent gives SAYS NOTHING, and a bracket
 that answers over a zero gives ZERO-ON-LIVE-PATH.

 THE ABLATION PASSED AND THE WORDS WERE STILL WRONG, which is the part worth
 keeping. Four reachable, distinct branches say the decision is not degenerate;
 they say nothing about whether each branch's SENTENCE is supported by what it
 read. Two of them claimed a hole from a zero, and the refutation was sitting
 in this chapter's own prose. A reachability ablation cannot catch that, and
 reading it as if it could is what let the overclaim ship.

 | reading | values measured | arm that moves it |
 |---|---|---|
 | 770.17 raw | 4000 / 0000 / ERR | `-i219`, `-i219-k1-nvm 0`, plain `-e1000` |
 | K1 decode | moves with the raw value | the same three |
 | stage state | ok / no-mdio | the same three |
 | `bmsr` | 0000 / ERR | `-e1000-phy-err` |
 | MDIO gate 769.16 | 2180 / ERR | `-e1000-phy-err` |
 | ULP row | three verdicts, see the control below | `-e1000-phy-err` |
 | DMAR | n / y | `-dmar` |
 | PCI command | 0007 bm=y / 0003 bm=n | `-nic-bme-clear` |
 | EXTCNF 0x00F00 | 00000000 / 00000080 | `-i219-mng-holds` |
 | kmrn 0x00034 + bracket | **DOES NOT MOVE: 00000000 in every arm** | none exists, see below |

 THE LAST THREE ROWS ONLY BECAME PROVABLE BECAUSE THE BED GREW FOR THEM. On the
 first scoring DMAR, the command word and EXTCNF were constants and carried an
 `[unproven]` mark in the painted row; reek landed `-dmar`, `-nic-bme-clear` and
 pointed out that EXTCNF moves only when firmware holds the semaphore
 (`-i219-mng-holds`), and the marks came off because they had stopped being
 true. A caveat kept after it is false is worse than none: it trains its reader
 to skip caveats.

 THE DMAR PAIR IS ALSO THE ONLY THING PROVING THE WALK. reek: no ACPI walker
 exists in this tree and this stage is its first reader (L-UNCALLED), so `n` in
 every arm would have been indistinguishable from a walk that never finds
 anything. The table the bed publishes is header only, which suits a reader that
 matches a signature and never touches the body.

 ULP CANNOT BE DONE THIS WAY AT ALL, AND GOT SOMETHING BETTER. reek 2026-08-21:
 the ULP exit sequence is unspecified in the documents we hold, so no bed arm for
 it can ever exist, which makes a BOARD reading the only evidence obtainable
 anywhere; red promoted it from one of eight to the row the flight exists to
 get. A row that can never be proven in the bed and is also the most important
 row is the worst possible thing to ship with a shrug, so it gets the neighbour
 control below.

 ULP GETS A NEIGHBOUR CONTROL (red, 2026-08-21), and it is
 the better instrument: it converts an unprovable read into a provable one using
 the board itself rather than the bed. A KNOWN-VALUE register is read on the
 same page selection and the same MDIO path, immediately before the target.
 PHY ID1 is register 2, and registers 0 to 15 are the IEEE set which is the same
 in every page (`E1000e.codex`), so it is reachable while page 779 is selected
 and its value does not depend on the page.

 The three outcomes are distinguishable, which is the whole point:
 neighbour answers and ULP does not, so the ULP error is REAL and about that
 register; both fail, so the PATH is down and the ULP error says nothing about
 ULP; both answer, so the ULP value is a reading taken on a path shown to work
 in the same breath.

 MEASURED, and this row therefore passes the reading gate on its CONTROL where
 its value never could. `-i219` and plain `-e1000` both answer
 `neighbour-id1=0154` with ULP at ERR, so the row says the ULP error is REAL:
 the bed models the page-770 register and not this one, and the instrument
 proves that rather than leaving it ambiguous. `-e1000-phy-err` fails the page
 select and the row says nothing on the page was reached. Three distinguishable
 answers from an unprovable read.


 EXTCNF_CTRL = MAC CSR 0x00F00, and the caveat travels with the number: that is
 the 82583V rev 2.6 section 9.2.2.15 map, and the I219's MAC is not a discrete
 controller but sits in the PCH, whose CSR map is in no document here. So it is
 CORROBORATED BY FAMILY, NOT CITED FOR THE PART. Bit 5 is MDIO/NVM SW Ownership,
 which is SWFLAG; bit 6 is HW ownership and bit 7 is MNG.

  dpch-extcnf-ctrl : Integer = #00F00
  dpch-swflag-bit : Integer = #00000020

 ULP Configuration 1 is PHY page 779 register 16, I219 rev 2.02 section 9.5.7.1,
 and this one IS cited for the part. Bit 2 is ULP_IND, "Power Up from ULP
 indication", which is the state bit the campaign asks about.

  dpch-ulp-page : Integer = 779
  dpch-ulp-reg : Integer = 16
  dpch-ulp-ind-bit : Integer = 4

 PCIe Power Management Control is PHY page 770 register 17, CITED, I219 rev 2.02
 section 9.5.5.2. Bit 13 is Giga_K1_disable ("When set, the I219 does not enter
 K1 while link speed at 1000 Mb/s") and bit 14 is K1 enable; both RW, both
 defaulting to 0b. THIS IS THE ROW THE FLIGHT IS FOR: the power-up value is the
 platform's NVM setting, which we do not have for this board, and if it already
 reads K1 disabled then the K1 theory dies on one register read rather than on a
 driver rewrite.

  dpch-k1-page : Integer = 770
  dpch-k1-reg : Integer = 17
  dpch-k1-disable-bit : Integer = #2000
  dpch-k1-enable-bit : Integer = #4000

 MDIO IS GATED AND THE GATE IS ITSELF A READING. I219 rev 2.02 section 9.2:
 access using MDIO should be done only when bit 10 in page 769 register 16 is
 set. THE GATE IS PAINTED AS A FACT AND DOES NOT SUPPRESS THE VALUES, because
 the flight's whole job is to bring a raw reading back for a human to judge and
 a stage that withholds the number decides that for them. The state says
 `no-mdio` only when the paged read ITSELF fails to answer, which is a different
 claim from the door being shut: measured in the bed, the door reads shut
 (769.16 = 0x2180, bit 10 clear) and page 770 register 17 answers anyway, so
 treating the bit as a veto would have thrown away the one reading this stage
 exists for.

  dpch-gate-page : Integer = 769
  dpch-gate-reg : Integer = 16
  dpch-gate-bit : Integer = #0400

 KMRNCTRLSTA IS READ RAW AND SO IS ITS BRACKET, because the question is whether
 the part decodes MAC CSR 0x00034 AT ALL. reek and blu found independently that
 the 82583V rev 2.6 map has a HOLE there, between FCT at 0x00030 and VET at
 0x00038. That would change a verdict rather than add a number: if the part
 reads a hole, a kumeran write to it is inert rather than merely untested. The
 row brings back the evidence for that question. It does not answer it.

 A ZERO AT 0x00034 ON ITS OWN CANNOT SAY THAT, which is why the bracket is read
 in the same breath. An unimplemented register inside a decoded BAR reads zero
 and so does an idle implemented one. FCT and VET sit either side of it on the
 same BAR and the same path, so a bracket that answers proves THE PATH IS LIVE:
 the BAR decodes, MMIO is returning real data, and a zero at 0x00034 is a real
 reading rather than an artefact of a dead window.

 THE BRACKET PROVES THE PATH, NOT THE OFFSET, and this row claimed otherwise
 until 2026-08-21. It read a live bracket over a zero as HOLE and said the
 kumeran write is INERT, which the paragraph above refutes in its own second
 sentence: an idle implemented register reads zero on a live path too. red
 caught it against the board's own numbers before it was routed onward, which
 would have told another lane its work was pointless on evidence that does not
 carry that. A PASSIVE READ CANNOT SEPARATE A HOLE FROM AN IDLE REGISTER.

 WHAT WOULD SEPARATE THEM IS A WRITE AND A READ BACK AT 0x00034, AND IT IS
 RULED OUT (red, 2026-08-21). Do not re-propose it from this row. It is an
 active write to an offset we cannot cite on the only board we have, and
 NOTHING DEPENDS ON THE ANSWER: the campaign moved to the cited 770.17 and
 kumeran is disabled either way, so the write would buy a fact no decision is
 waiting on. That is the whole test, and it is worth stating in those terms
 rather than as a safety rule -- risk is not the reason on its own, an
 unbought answer is. Revisit only if 770.17 proves insufficient; the question
 then has a decision behind it and earns the risk it costs.

 THE BED CANNOT MOVE THIS ROW, and it is marked in the painted row rather than
 left for a reader to find. codex-vm backs the whole of BAR0 with a flat array,
 so every offset decodes, none of these three has a modelled reset value, and
 all three read 00000000 in every arm. That is the always-one-word instrument
 this chapter refuses everywhere else, so it is declared: the board is the only
 place this row can move, exactly as the ULP row above.

  dpch-fct-off : Integer = #00030
  dpch-kmrn-off : Integer = #00034
  dpch-vet-off : Integer = #00038

 The PCI command register is config offset 4, low sixteen bits, and bit 2 is bus
 master. That is architectural rather than part-specific.

  dpch-cmd-off : Integer = 4
  dpch-cmd-bm : Integer = #0004
  dpch-cmd-mem : Integer = #0002

Section: Reads

  dpch-paged : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dpch-paged (mmio) (page) (reg) =
   let p = e1000-phy-set-page mmio page
   in if p < 0 then 0 - 1
   else let v = e1000-phy-read mmio reg
   in let back = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0
   in v

  dpch-hex : Integer -> Text
  dpch-hex (v) = if v < 0 then "ERR" else diag-hex4 v

 THE VERDICT NAMES ITS OWN REGISTER since 2026-08-21, and it did not before.
 The words were written for the ULP row and read correctly there; the moment a
 second row shared the control, the SMBus row printed "so the ULP ERR is REAL"
 about register 23, which is a sentence that names the wrong subject in the
 one place a reader is trusting it most. Caught by running the row rather than
 by reading it.

  dpch-path-verdict : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  dpch-path-verdict (ctl) (v) (who) =
   if ctl < 0 then "BROKEN both reads failed, so this ERR is about the PATH and says nothing about " & who
   else if v < 0 then "OK neighbour answered and " & who & " did not, so the " & who & " ERR is REAL"
   else "OK neighbour answered in the same page selection"

  dpch-ulp-row : Integer -> Text
  dpch-ulp-row (mmio) =
   let p = e1000-phy-set-page mmio dpch-ulp-page
   in if p < 0 then "ulp 779.16=ERR page-select FAILED, so nothing on this page was reached"
   else let ctl = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-id1
   in let v = e1000-phy-read mmio dpch-ulp-reg
   in let back = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0
   in "ulp 779.16=" & dpch-hex v & " ulp-ind=" & dpch-bit v dpch-ulp-ind-bit & " neighbour-id1=" & dpch-hex ctl & " path=" & dpch-path-verdict ctl v "ULP"

 THE SMBus CONTROL REGISTER IS READ AND NOT WRITTEN, and the split is the same
 one ULP turned out to have. I219 rev 2.02 section 9.5.3.4 puts it at PHY page
 769 register 23 and names its fields: Force SMBus is bit 0, "Force SMBus,
 reset on PCI reset de-assertion"; dis_SMB_filtering is bit 1; Use LANWAKE# is
 bit 4. So the register IS cited for the part, unlike EXTCNF beside it.

 WHY THE ROW STOPS AT READING. Forcing SMBus moves the LAN Connected Device
 onto the SMBus path, which is the path our MDIO access is not on: a driver
 that forces it can take its own access to the part away, and sitting 10 has
 bring-up hanging inside `e1000-reset` for reasons not yet named. A read costs
 nothing and answers the question the write would be guessing at -- whether
 this part is in SMBus mode at all -- and nobody has ever read it. The board
 value is UNKNOWN, so this row's first flight is a reading and not a check.

 It shares the neighbour control rather than repeating it: page 769 is where
 the MDIO gate at 769.16 already lives, so a failed read here is separable
 from a dead path by the same PHY ID1 read the ULP row uses.

  dpch-smbus-page : Integer = 769
  dpch-smbus-reg : Integer = 23
  dpch-smbus-force-bit : Integer = #0001
  dpch-smbus-nofilter-bit : Integer = #0002
  dpch-smbus-lanwake-bit : Integer = #0010

  dpch-smbus-row : Integer -> Text
  dpch-smbus-row (mmio) =
   let p = e1000-phy-set-page mmio dpch-smbus-page
   in if p < 0 then "smbus 769.23=ERR page-select FAILED, so nothing on this page was reached"
   else let ctl = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-id1
   in let v = e1000-phy-read mmio dpch-smbus-reg
   in let back = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0
   in "smbus 769.23=" & dpch-hex v & " force=" & dpch-bit v dpch-smbus-force-bit & " nofilter=" & dpch-bit v dpch-smbus-nofilter-bit & " lanwake=" & dpch-bit v dpch-smbus-lanwake-bit & " neighbour-id1=" & dpch-hex ctl & " path=" & dpch-path-verdict ctl v "SMBUS"

  dpch-bit : Integer, Integer -> Text
  dpch-bit (v) (mask) = if v < 0 then "?" else na-yn (bit-and v mask)

 ALL-ONES IS NOT A VALUE. A read that nothing claims comes back FFFFFFFF on this
 bus, so treating non-zero as "it answered" would report the strongest possible
 evidence of a hole as proof of the opposite.

  dpch-ones : Integer = #FFFFFFFF

  dpch-answered : Integer -> Boolean
  dpch-answered (v) = v /= 0 & v /= dpch-ones

  dpch-kmrn-verdict : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  dpch-kmrn-verdict (fct) (kmrn) (vet) =
   if dpch-answered kmrn then "DECODES 0x00034 answered with a value, so something is implemented there"
   else if kmrn == dpch-ones then "UNCLAIMED 0x00034 reads all-ones, which is a read nothing answered rather than a value"
   else if (dpch-answered fct == False) & (dpch-answered vet == False) then "SAYS NOTHING the bracket did not answer either, so the path itself is unproven and the zero means nothing"
   else "ZERO-ON-LIVE-PATH the bracket answered so the window decodes and this zero is real, but a hole and an idle register both read zero and a passive read cannot separate them"

  dpch-kmrn-row : Integer -> Text
  dpch-kmrn-row (mmio) =
   let fct = e1000-read mmio dpch-fct-off
   in let kmrn = e1000-read mmio dpch-kmrn-off
   in let vet = e1000-read mmio dpch-vet-off
   in "kmrn 0x00034=" & diag-hex8 kmrn & " fct 0x00030=" & diag-hex8 fct & " vet 0x00038=" & diag-hex8 vet & " " & dpch-kmrn-verdict fct kmrn vet & " (bed cannot move this row, board only)"

 DMAR present or absent is the IOMMU question and it is one table lookup. The
 signature bytes are 'D' 'M' 'A' 'R'. A firmware that publishes no DMAR is
 telling us there is no VT-d unit to have blocked anything.

  dpch-dmar : Integer -> Text
  dpch-dmar (dummy) =
   let rsdp = acpi-boot-rsdp 0
   in if rsdp == 0 then "no-rsdp"
   else let root = acpi-root-of rsdp
   in if root == 0 then "no-root"
   else let entsize = acpi-entsize-of root
   in let n = acpi-entry-count root entsize
   in if acpi-find root entsize n 0 68 77 65 82 == 0 then "n" else "y"

Section: Rows

  dpch-bdf : PciDevice -> Text
  dpch-bdf (d) = show (d.pci-bus) & ":" & show (d.pci-dev) & "." & show (d.pci-func)

  dpch-part : PciDevice -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] DiagResult
  dpch-part (d) =
   if na-eligible d == False then diag-result "rejected" diag-col-warn ["part " & dpch-bdf d & " " & na-reject-line d] ["part " & dpch-bdf d & " " & na-reject-line d]
   else let v = e1000-bar-verdict (na-bar-of d)
   in if v /= e1000-bar-ok then diag-result "bar-bad" diag-col-bad ["part " & dpch-bdf d & " verdict=" & na-verdict-text v] ["part " & dpch-bdf d & " verdict=" & na-verdict-text v]
   else let mmio = na-mmio-of d
   in let cmd = bit-and (pci-read-config (d.pci-bus) (d.pci-dev) (d.pci-func) dpch-cmd-off) #FFFF
   in let extcnf = e1000-read mmio dpch-extcnf-ctrl
   in let gate = dpch-paged mmio dpch-gate-page dpch-gate-reg
   in let k1 = dpch-paged mmio dpch-k1-page dpch-k1-reg
   in let bmsr = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-phy-bmsr
   in let dmar = dpch-dmar 0
   in let open = if gate < 0 then False else bit-and gate dpch-gate-bit /= 0
   in let r1 = "pch " & dpch-bdf d & " cmd=" & diag-hex4 cmd & " bm=" & na-yn (bit-and cmd dpch-cmd-bm) & " mem=" & na-yn (bit-and cmd dpch-cmd-mem) & " dmar=" & dmar
   in let r2 = "k1 770.17=" & dpch-hex k1 & " giga-k1-dis=" & dpch-bit k1 dpch-k1-disable-bit & " k1-en=" & dpch-bit k1 dpch-k1-enable-bit
   in let r3 = dpch-ulp-row mmio & " mdio-gate 769.16=" & dpch-hex gate & " open=" & na-yn (if open then 1 else 0)
   in let r4 = "extcnf 0x00F00=" & diag-hex8 extcnf & " swflag=" & na-yn (bit-and extcnf dpch-swflag-bit) & " bmsr=" & dpch-hex bmsr & " (extcnf offset family-corroborated, NOT cited for the part)"
   in let rs = dpch-smbus-row mmio
   in let rk = dpch-kmrn-row mmio
   in let r5 = "fwsm not painted: no cited offset in either datasheet here, and the vendor driver is the one source this campaign forbids"
   in if k1 < 0 then diag-result "no-mdio" diag-col-warn [r1, r2, r3] [r1, r2, r3, r4, rs, rk, r5]
   else diag-result "ok" diag-col-good [r1, r2, r3] [r1, r2, r3, r4, rs, rk, r5]

  dpch-run : DiagCtx -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] DiagResult
  dpch-run (c) =
   when e1000-find (c.dc-pci)
    is None -> diag-result "no-part" diag-col-dim ["no eligible Intel part on the bus"] ["no eligible Intel part on the bus"]
    is Just (d) -> dpch-part d
